import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Licensing — FomaDev"
  description="FomaDev projects are released under one of two licenses: the Fordima Public License (FPL) or the Fordima Commercial License (FCL)."
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">
    <a href="/" class="text-sm text-zinc-500 hover:text-zinc-300">← All projects</a>

    <div class="prose prose-invert prose-emerald mt-8 max-w-none">
      <h1>Licensing</h1>

      <p>
        Everything built within the Fordima ecosystem is released under one of
        two licenses. They share one idea: <strong>people should be free to use
        our software, and the work behind it should stay protected.</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        Fordima Inc. is the parent company. Its subsidiaries, such as FomaDev,
        build and publish the software. The licenses belong to Fordima, so
        every project follows the same clear rules, whichever team made it.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2 id="fpl">Fordima Public License (FPL)</h2>

      <p>
        The FPL is our license for open, collaborative software. Most of our
        projects use it.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>What it is.</strong> The source code is public and free to use.
        It is <em>source-available</em>: you can read it, learn from it and
        contribute to it, while a few specific uses stay reserved, to protect
        the project.
      </p>

      <p><strong>What it gives you.</strong></p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Free use, including in business.</strong> Individuals,
          students and companies can use FPL software at no cost, including in
          commercial products and projects that earn money. No fee, no royalty,
          no registration.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Open contributions.</strong> You can fork a project, improve
          it and send your work back through a pull request. Contributions are
          welcome and make the project better for everyone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Freedom to integrate.</strong> You can build parts of the
          code into your own systems and tools.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <strong>What it reserves.</strong> A paid license is required only when
        the code is used to create a similar or competing product, to keep a
        private copy to build the same thing, to resell the source code, or to
        offer a paid hosted version of it. Permanent forks made to bypass the
        official project are not allowed.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Why it matters.</strong> It keeps our tools accessible to
        everyone, from a student learning in Kinshasa to a large company
        shipping a product, while making sure that the people who build and
        maintain a project are not simply copied and replaced by a competitor
        using their own code. That protection is what lets us keep publishing,
        improving and supporting open software.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2 id="fcl">Fordima Commercial License (FCL)</h2>

      <p>
        The FCL is our license for paid software. It covers the commercial
        editions of our products, such as Pro and Cloud versions.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>What it is.</strong> The source may be available for
        evaluation, but the software is not open source. You buy the right to
        <em>use</em> it; you do not buy the code itself.
      </p>

      <p><strong>What it gives you.</strong></p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Free evaluation.</strong> You can install and test the
          software locally or in staging, without paying and without a license
          key, to check that it fits your needs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Production rights.</strong> A paid license lets you run the
          software in production: in a deployed website, a live API or a
          service for your own clients, according to the plan you chose.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Professional support and updates</strong> for the duration of
          your plan.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <strong>What it reserves.</strong> The software cannot be resold,
        redistributed, sublicensed or used to build a competing product.
        License keys cannot be shared.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Why it matters.</strong> Paid products fund the full ecosystem:
        the commercial editions pay for the time spent on the free ones. They
        also give businesses a clear contract, with defined rights, support and
        responsibilities, and that is what they need to rely on software in
        production.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>How the two work together</h2>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th></th>
            <th>FPL</th>
            <th>FCL</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Purpose</strong></td>
            <td>Open, collaborative software</td>
            <td>Paid, commercial software</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Cost</strong></td>
            <td>Free, including for businesses</td>
            <td>Paid for production use</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Source code</strong></td>
            <td>Public, open to contributions</td>
            <td>Available for evaluation only</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Contributions</strong></td>
            <td>Welcome via pull request</td>
            <td>Not applicable</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Competing products</strong></td>
            <td>Require a paid license</td>
            <td>Not permitted</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        A single product can use both: an FPL core that everyone can use and
        improve, and an FCL edition with advanced features for businesses that
        need them.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>Commercial licensing</h2>

      <p>
        To obtain a commercial license or discuss a custom agreement, please
        contact the management of Fordima Inc.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>